    "turn_idx": 417,
    "turn_text": "big implications. We've really withdrawn in terms of Africa. We're more than a year into this administration, and we still haven't even nominated Assistant Secretary for Africa over at the State Department. Ambassador ships are being left vacant, important ones. And more than that, some of the programs that we have that are extremely important in terms of building the kind of trust that we need in these countries so that we can have security relationships, intelligence sharing. I know you've talked about before programs like PEPFAR. Extremely important in Africa. In fact, I was two years ago. It's a program that the Bush administration launched. That's right. The Obama administration continued with it, and I actually met with Robert Mugabe two years ago. I'd written my master's thesis on McGauvey, you know, 30 years before trying to explain his hold on power. He was still there. We met with him, and he was railing against every American president and British Prime Minister from the Lancaster House of 1980 on through. And when he got to George W. Bush, I stopped him and said, hey, it's a bit unfair. PEPFAR really helped you guys. Without question. And I'll never forget, he stopped. You know, the worst dictator in Africa, McGauvey, and said, I'll give you that. That was a pretty stark admission, but that's helped in other countries as well to have the kind of security arrangements that we need to benefit the United States and to serve our national interests in addition to the most important thing, alleviating a lot of human suffering there.",
